  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "concat_function",
    "file": "php-src/Zend/zend_operators.c",
    "description": "The compound-concatenation implementation does not safely account for storage aliasing where `result == op1` but `op2` is a distinct zval that references the same `zend_string`. In the non-empty-string path, `zend_string_extend()` may replace/reallocate the result string and the subsequent copy at line 1982 still reads through `op2`; the existing comment/ordering only handles literal zval identity (`result == op1 == op2`), not equal `Z_STR_P()` pointers in different zvals. This can leave corrupted/dangling string state in a CV/symbol-table entry; the observed read in `zend_gc_delref()` occurs later while the executor destroys that entry.",
    "conditions": "Execute attacker-controlled PHP containing a nested self compound concatenation whose inner result is consumed by an outer concatenation of the same variable, e.g. the loop condition `$i .= $i .= 0`. The inner `ZEND_ASSIGN_OP` updates `$i` and returns a copied temporary, so the outer `ZEND_ASSIGN_OP` calls `concat_function(&i, &i, temp)` with distinct zvals sharing the same string. Repetition rapidly grows the string, exercising replacement/reallocation of the aliased backing allocation. The subsequent `$$i++` uses the resulting string as a dynamic-variable name and propagates the bad state into the executor symbol table. Request shutdown then destructs that table and dereferences the invalid refcounted pointer in `zend_gc_delref`."
  },
